iphlpapi: deliver NotifyIpInterfaceChange / NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange callbacks

Windows' NotifyIpInterfaceChange() and NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange() register a callback
that the system invokes whenever an IP interface or a unicast IP address is added, removed
or modified, and return a handle that CancelMibChangeNotify2() unregisters.  Wine implemented
neither: NotifyIpInterfaceChange() was a pure stub that returned NO_ERROR, set
*handle = NULL and never invoked the callback, and NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange() was a
semi-stub that only called back when InitialNotification != 0.  An application that registers
for change notifications (ETC Eos' EtcPal NetworkMonitor does, with InitialNotification
FALSE for both) therefore never saw a single notification under Wine.

The implementation follows the existing NSI change-notification plumbing already used by
NotifyAddrChange(): a per-registration worker thread keeps an asynchronous
NsiRequestChangeNotification() on NSI_IP_UNICAST_TABLE armed for AF_INET, AF_INET6, or both
families for AF_UNSPEC.  An NSI notification only reports the module/table that changed, not
what changed in it, so on every wake (and on a 1 s timeout) the relevant table
(GetIpInterfaceTable() / GetUnicastIpAddressTable()) is re-enumerated and diffed against the
previous snapshot; the differences are reported to the callback as MibAddInstance /
MibDeleteInstance / MibParameterNotification with a pointer to the row.  If
InitialNotification is TRUE the callback is invoked synchronously with a NULL row and
MibInitialNotification, exactly as Windows documents.  CancelMibChangeNotify2() stops the
worker thread, cancels the pending NSI requests and frees the registration; the handle is an
opaque per-registration pointer, so (as on Windows) CloseHandle() on it fails.

Deliberate simplification: the NSI plumbing has no notification source for the interface
table (the Linux netlink socket is subscribed to RTMGRP_IPV4_IFADDR/RTMGRP_IPV6_IFADDR
only), so interface changes that do not also modify the unicast address table (pure link
state, MTU, ...) are detected by the periodic re-check rather than being signalled
immediately.  Address changes are still signalled by NSI.  AF_UNSPEC registers both families.

Verification: with this patch the Eos start-up trace should show
iphlpapi:NotifyIpInterfaceChange / NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange being registered with a
non-NULL handle instead of the former fixme: stub / semi-stub messages, and any interface or
address change (e.g. adding an address with `ip addr add` on the host while Eos runs) should
produce the corresponding callback.  Wine's own dlls/iphlpapi/tests/iphlpapi.c
test_NotifyUnicastIpAddressChange (initial notification, then CancelMibChangeNotify2) must
keep passing.  Reaching the Eos editor in ~10 s like Windows additionally needs the bundle's
slpd SLP service in the prefix, which the discovery bring-up in the same window depends on.

